From: B Honey <9922368+Mhsbrian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 21:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] OPENBOOK: Meta+1..9 are taskbar shortcuts

Meta+N opens the Nth app on the taskbar, brings its window forward, or
minimizes the window in front, as on other desktop systems. The keys are
system shortcuts, so they work while an app has keyboard focus, and the
system reports each press once: a held key does not repeat the gesture.

The shortcuts report one OpenBook gesture type, 10001, with the digit's
key code. OpenBook Launcher handles it through MANAGE_KEY_GESTURES, which
the recents role grants. Without a handler the keys do nothing.
An app that captures the keyboard keeps Meta+N.
---
 .../server/input/InputGestureManager.java       | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/input/InputGestureManager.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/input/InputGestureManager.java
index 2ba2745..d9e046d 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/input/InputGestureManager.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/input/InputGestureManager.java
@@ -65,6 +65,14 @@ final class InputGestureManager {
             KeyEvent.META_CTRL_ON | KeyEvent.META_ALT_ON | KeyEvent.META_SHIFT_ON
                     | KeyEvent.META_META_ON;
 
+    /**
+     * OPENBOOK: Meta+1…9 act on the Nth app on the taskbar: open it, bring its window forward,
+     * or minimize the window in front. OpenBook Launcher handles this gesture type
+     * (TaskbarAppShortcuts) and reads N from the digit's key code. OpenBook's own types start
+     * at 10001, well clear of AOSP's (they end at 90 in android17).
+     */
+    static final int KEY_GESTURE_TYPE_OPENBOOK_TASKBAR_APP = 10001;
+
     private final Context mContext;
 
     private static final Object mGestureLock = new Object();
@@ -402,6 +410,15 @@ final class InputGestureManager {
                             KeyGestureEvent.KEY_GESTURE_TYPE_LAUNCH_CONTEXTUAL_CURSOR,
                             /* allowCaptureByFocusedWindow= */ true));
         }
+        // OPENBOOK: Meta+1…9, the taskbar's apps. An app that captures the keyboard keeps them.
+        for (int keycode = KeyEvent.KEYCODE_1; keycode <= KeyEvent.KEYCODE_9; keycode++) {
+            systemShortcuts.add(
+                    createKeyGesture(
+                            keycode,
+                            KeyEvent.META_META_ON,
+                            KEY_GESTURE_TYPE_OPENBOOK_TASKBAR_APP,
+                            /* allowCaptureByFocusedWindow= */ true));
+        }
         synchronized (mGestureLock) {
             for (InputGestureData systemShortcut : systemShortcuts) {
                 mSystemShortcuts.put(systemShortcut.getTrigger(), systemShortcut);
